  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Saturday)\nIssued at 101 AM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nLong-term forecast impacts: Warming temperatures through the week \nwith low rain chances Thursday/Friday, then a more impactful frontal \nsystem possible over the weekend.\n\nDiscussion: The large-scale pattern will be dominated by \nsouthwesterly mid-level flow for multiple days during the \nmiddle/latter part of the week. We're moving well into the cool \nseason now, so that flow regime will feature at least one and \npotentially more well-defined disturbances pushing through the \nPlains. At first, rain chances will be rather slim with those \ndisturbances, as this current storm system will hinder efficient \nmoisture return. Still, by late in the week, multiple days of return \nflow are likely to bring warmer temperatures in the 60s and 70s and \ngreater boundary-layer moisture. Therefore, those same disturbances \nby Thursday and especially Friday will have a greater chance of \nleading to showers or storms.\n\nGuidance continues to indicate a more forceful trough/ejection \npassage on either Saturday or Sunday. This trough will include a \nsignificant frontal passage and likely a chance of pre-or-post-\nfrontal storms. Confidence in the exact evolution remains low, so \nstay tuned for the latest forecast updates.\n\nMeister",
